\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-d3, P: c2-c3, K: e1-d1, P: e4-e5, B: e8-d7, B: e8-c6, B: e8-b5, B: e8-a4, B: e8-f7, B: e8-g6, B: e8-h5, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White and analyzing the current position. Black's king is on f8, and Black has a queen on b8, rook on a8, bishop on e8, knight on h5, and several pawns. My pieces include both rooks on a1 and h1, a knight on b1, my king on e1, and a bishop on e8 (which appears to be a promoted piece deep in Black's position). I have a pawn on e4 that's advanced. Black's king appears somewhat exposed with limited escape squares.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most promising moves:\n\n1. **B: e8-f7** - This bishop move would give check to the Black king on f8, forcing it to move. This is very strong as it puts immediate pressure on the exposed king.\n\n2. **B: e8-d7** - Controls central squares but doesn't create immediate threats.\n\n3. **P: e4-e5** - Advances the passed pawn but doesn't create immediate tactical opportunities.\n\n4. **N: b1-c3** - Develops the knight but is slow given the tactical opportunity available.\n\n5. **P: f2-f4** - Supports potential kingside expansion but doesn't address the immediate tactical situation.\n\nReason: B: e8-f7 is clearly the best move. It delivers check to the Black king on f8, which must immediately respond. The king has very limited options - it likely must move to e7 or g8 (need to verify f7 isn't protected, but since my bishop would be giving check from f7, the king can't capture it if I'm moving there legally). This check creates immediate tactical pressure and disrupts Black's coordination. When you have a forcing move like check that improves your position, it's almost always the right choice, especially when the enemy king is exposed.\n\nAnswer: B: e8-f7"}
